  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a controlled comparison of two specific cultivars of a single tree species, which may not represent all red-leafed urban tree varieties.",
      "what": "This study compared the physiological responses of red-leafed ('Crimson King') and green-leafed ('Summershade') cultivars of Acer platanoides under chronic light stress, simulated using light-coloured concrete slabs, and a one-month drought period. The red-leafed cultivar maintained a higher photosynthetic light saturation point and higher net photosynthesis during peak daylight hours under both light and drought stress compared to its green-leafed counterpart.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that anthocyanin-rich, red-leafed trees are physiologically better adapted to the high-reflectance and drought-prone environments of paved urban plazas, though they face carbon gain limitations under natural, unreflected irradiance."
    }
  },
